<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Samples.HelloUi</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>HelloUi</AssemblyName>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

        <!--
            ⚠ **This one line is the whole of the UI plumbing, and it used to be forty.** It says
            "this project has an interface in it", and what arrives is the VXML compiler, the
            `[UiProperty]` generator, the `.vxml` and `.vcss` item types and the utility-stylesheet
            step. Outside this repository it is not even a line: `Vixen.Ui` ships its build assets in
            `buildTransitive/`, so a `PackageReference` anywhere in the graph brings all of it. In
            here there are no packages and analyzers do not travel through a `ProjectReference`, so
            `Directory.Build.targets` stands in for one.

            Adding a second `.vxml` beside `Shell.vxml`, or a second `.vcss` beside the theme, is not
            a project-file change.
        -->
        <VixenUi>true</VixenUi>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **No Vixen.Engine and no Vixen.App, and that absence is the sample.**
            docs/plan/02 § Samples calls this one "Vixen.Ui only, no engine — proves the UI/Engine
            boundary", and doc 15 makes it what proves the framework standalone before the editor is
            allowed to depend on it. `CheckArchitecture` asserts the absence, so it cannot be undone
            by accident.

            ⚠ **`Vixen.Ui.Desktop` is not a way round that rule and is the reason the rule is now
            cheap to keep.** It is a Platform/ assembly whose whole content is a window, a Vulkan
            device and the four steps of a frame; it references no scene, no ECS world and no game
            loop. Until it existed, an application that wanted a window had to choose between writing
            four hundred lines of swapchain handling — which is what this sample's Program.cs used to
            be — and referencing a host that drags an engine behind it. Everything that file did is
            `UiApplication` now, and Program.cs is thirty lines.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Ui.Desktop\Vixen.Ui.Desktop.csproj" />

        <!--
            Docking, trees and property grids, which are a second package on purpose: an application
            that is one window of controls should not link a virtualiser. `AdvancedTheme.Install` is
            one line in Program.cs, in the assembly that references it.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced\Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced.csproj" />

        <!--
            ⚠ Dev-only, and referenced here for what the sample is *for*: editing `Theme/shell.vcss`
            with the window open and watching the interface change is the authoring loop this sample
            exists to demonstrate. A shipping application drops this reference and the six lines in
            Program.cs that use it — see `Vixen.Ui.HotReload`'s own project file, which says why it is
            neither trimmable nor AOT-compatible.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.HotReload\Vixen.Ui.HotReload.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **The sample's own rules, handed to the utility step as its base sheet rather than
            concatenated afterwards.** `shell.vcss` opens with `@layer base, components, utilities;`
            and that statement is what fixes the ladder's order, so it has to be emitted *first*: the
            generated utilities name `utilities` too, and written first they would make it layer zero
            and let a `@layer components` rule declared later beat them. StyleGen puts every base
            sheet ahead of what it generates for exactly this reason, and expands the sheet's
            `@apply` on the way through.

            `Theme/vixen.ui.vcss` is not listed, and must not be: it is the *palette*, found by name
            rather than by item, and naming it here would emit the `@theme` block as ordinary rules
            as well as reading it as tokens.
        -->
        <VixenStyleBase Include="Theme/shell.vcss" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
